/* The Article Machine — data-plain-5.js
   Parts XII to XXII — finance and property, trade and commerce, the services,
   tribunals, elections, special provisions for certain classes, the official
   language, the emergency provisions, the miscellaneous articles, the power of
   amendment, the temporary and transitional provisions, and the closing Part. */

Object.assign(window.PLAIN, {

/* --------- PART XII — FINANCE, PROPERTY, CONTRACTS AND SUITS --------- */

"264": {p:"“Finance Commission” in this Part means a Commission constituted under article 280."},

"265": {p:"No tax shall be levied or collected except by authority of law.",
 w:"Eleven words that make every tax in India traceable to a statute. An executive order cannot impose a tax, and a tax collected without legal authority must be refunded. Note that it says “law”, not “Act of Parliament” — a validly made rule under a statute counts."},

"266": {p:"All revenues of the Union go into the Consolidated Fund of India, and of a State into that State's Consolidated Fund. Other public money goes into the public account. No money may leave the Consolidated Fund except as the Constitution allows."},

"267": {p:"Parliament and each State legislature may create a Contingency Fund, placed at the disposal of the President or Governor, to meet unforeseen expenditure pending authorisation by the legislature.",
 w:"An advance, not a grant: whatever is taken out must be recouped from the Consolidated Fund once the legislature approves."},

"268": {p:"Stamp duties mentioned in the Union List are levied by the Union but collected and kept by the States in which they are levied.",
 w:"The 101st Amendment removed excise duties on medicinal and toilet preparations from this article when GST subsumed them."},

"268A": {p:"Repealed. Inserted by the 88th Amendment in 2003 to give service tax its own constitutional head, levied by the Union and shared with the States. The 101st Amendment removed it in 2016, when GST absorbed service tax altogether."},

"269": {p:"Taxes on inter-State sale or consignment of goods are levied and collected by the Union but assigned to the States.",
 w:"Now largely of historical interest: article 269A took over the ground for goods and services tax."},

"269A": {p:"Goods and services tax on inter-State supply is levied and collected by the Union and shared between the Union and the States as Parliament provides on the GST Council's recommendation. Supply in the course of import counts as inter-State.",
 w:"Integrated GST lives here. Added by the 101st Amendment in 2016."},

"270": {p:"Taxes and duties in the Union List are levied and collected by the Union, and the States' share of the net proceeds is distributed as the President prescribes on the Finance Commission's recommendation.",
 w:"The main channel of fiscal federalism: this is the divisible pool. Surcharges and cesses under article 271 are excluded from it, which is why their growing share of central revenue is a live grievance among the States."},

"271": {p:"Parliament may increase any of these duties and taxes by a surcharge for Union purposes, and the whole proceeds go to the Union — except a surcharge on goods and services tax.",
 w:"The exclusion of surcharge proceeds from the divisible pool is the single most contested line in centre–State finance. Every Finance Commission since the Fourteenth has commented on it."},

"272": {p:"Repealed. Taxes levied and collected by the Union which might be distributed to the States. The 80th Amendment in 2000 replaced the article-by-article sharing system with the single divisible pool in article 270."},

"273": {p:"Grants in lieu of export duty on jute and jute products, charged on the Consolidated Fund of India and payable to Assam, Bihar, Odisha and West Bengal.",
 w:"A relic of Partition: the jute-growing districts stayed in India while the mills and the export trade were split. It was to last ten years or until Parliament provided otherwise."},

"274": {p:"A Bill that imposes or varies a tax in which States are interested, or that affects the principles of distribution to States, cannot be introduced without the President's recommendation."},

"275": {p:"Parliament may make grants-in-aid to States in need of assistance, charged on the Consolidated Fund of India — including specific grants for the welfare of Scheduled Tribes and the administration of Scheduled Areas.",
 w:"Statutory grants, distinct from the discretionary grants under article 282. The tribal-welfare proviso is the constitutional basis of central funding for Scheduled Areas."},

"276": {p:"A State or local authority may tax professions, trades, callings and employments, up to two thousand five hundred rupees a year per person — and such a tax is valid even though it relates to income.",
 w:"The ceiling was raised from two hundred and fifty rupees by the 60th Amendment in 1988, and has not moved since. It is the reason professional tax is a trivial sum in every State."},

"277": {p:"Taxes, duties, cesses and fees lawfully levied before the Constitution by a State or local authority may continue to be levied for the same purposes until Parliament provides otherwise."},

"278": {p:"Repealed by the 7th Amendment in 1956. It allowed agreements with the Part B States about finance, a category of State the same amendment abolished."},

"279": {p:"The “net proceeds” of a tax means its proceeds less the cost of collection, as certified by the Comptroller and Auditor-General, whose certificate is final."},

"279A": {p:"The President shall constitute a Goods and Services Tax Council — the Union Finance Minister, a Union Minister of State, and a minister from every State — to recommend the rates, exemptions, thresholds and model laws for GST. Decisions need a three-fourths majority of weighted votes, with the Union holding a third and the States two-thirds together.",
 w:"A genuinely novel institution: a permanent constitutional body in which the Union and the States must agree. The voting weights mean neither side can act alone — the Union cannot pass anything without some States, and the States cannot pass anything without the Union. In Mohit Minerals (2022) the Supreme Court held its recommendations are persuasive, not binding."},

"280": {p:"The President shall constitute a Finance Commission every five years — a chairman and four members — to recommend how the net proceeds of taxes should be divided between the Union and the States and among the States, the principles governing grants-in-aid, and measures to supplement the resources of panchayats and municipalities.",
 w:"The constitutional arbiter of fiscal federalism, and it is quasi-judicial rather than political. The last two clauses were added by the 73rd and 74th Amendments so that local bodies are not left out of the calculation."},

"281": {p:"The President must lay every recommendation of the Finance Commission, with an explanatory memorandum of the action taken on it, before both Houses of Parliament.",
 w:"The recommendations are not binding — but the government must explain in Parliament what it did with them, which in practice makes them very hard to refuse."},

"282": {p:"The Union or a State may make any grant for any public purpose, even one outside its own legislative competence.",
 w:"A short article with very long consequences. It is the constitutional basis of centrally sponsored schemes — the Union funding health, education and rural development, which are State subjects. Critics call it a route around the Seventh Schedule; the Court has upheld it, most recently in relation to Aadhaar-linked welfare."},

"283": {p:"The custody of the Consolidated Funds, Contingency Funds and public account moneys is regulated by law made by Parliament or the State legislature, and until then by rules made by the President or the Governor."},

"284": {p:"Money received by a public servant or a court on behalf of a suitor must be paid into the public account, not kept privately."},

"285": {p:"Union property is exempt from all taxes imposed by a State or by any authority within a State, unless Parliament provides otherwise."},

"286": {p:"A State cannot tax a sale or purchase of goods that takes place outside the State, or in the course of import into or export out of India.",
 w:"The article that keeps States from taxing each other's trade at the border. Recast by the 101st Amendment to fit the GST regime."},

"287": {p:"A State cannot tax the consumption or sale of electricity consumed by the Union, or consumed in the construction or operation of a railway."},

"288": {p:"A State cannot tax water or electricity stored, generated, consumed or distributed by an authority established by Parliament, unless the President assents to the law."},

"289": {p:"The property and income of a State are exempt from Union taxation — but the Union may tax a trade or business carried on by a State, unless Parliament declares it incidental to the ordinary functions of government.",
 w:"The mirror of article 285. Together they establish reciprocal immunity between the two levels of government — with the important exception that commercial activity is taxable."},

"290": {p:"Where the expenses of a court or Commission, or the pension of a person who served before the Constitution, are charged on one Consolidated Fund but relate to another government, the charge is apportioned as agreed or as an arbitrator appointed by the Chief Justice of India determines."},

"290A": {p:"Kerala must pay forty-six and a half lakh rupees a year to the Travancore Devaswom Fund, and Tamil Nadu thirteen and a half lakh to the Devaswom Fund for the temples in the transferred territories.",
 w:"The most oddly specific article in the Constitution — two fixed rupee sums, charged on the Consolidated Fund of a State in perpetuity. It preserves an obligation the princely state of Travancore had towards its temples when it merged into India."},

"291": {p:"Repealed. The privy purse: a tax-free annual payment to the rulers of the former princely states, guaranteed when they acceded to India. The 26th Amendment abolished it in 1971.",
 w:"The government first tried to abolish the privy purses by a presidential order in 1970; the Supreme Court struck that down in Madhav Rao Scindia. Parliament then did it by constitutional amendment instead."},

"292": {p:"The Union may borrow on the security of the Consolidated Fund of India, within limits Parliament may fix.",
 w:"Parliament has never fixed a limit. The Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act, 2003 is a statutory self-restraint, not a constitutional one."},

"293": {p:"A State may borrow within India on the security of its own Consolidated Fund — but if it owes anything to the Union, it cannot borrow without the Union's consent, which may be given on conditions.",
 w:"Two asymmetries here. States cannot borrow abroad at all, and because almost every State owes the Union something, in practice every State needs the centre's permission to borrow. It is one of the sharpest limits on State autonomy in the Constitution."},

"294": {p:"Property, assets, rights, liabilities and obligations of the old Provinces and Dominion pass to the corresponding States and to the Union."},

"295": {p:"The corresponding rule for the former princely states — their property, rights and liabilities pass to the Union or to the successor State."},

"296": {p:"Property that would have passed to the Crown by escheat, lapse or as unclaimed goods now passes to the State where it is situated, or to the Union if it was there for the purposes of the Union."},

"297": {p:"All lands, minerals and things of value beneath the territorial waters, the continental shelf and the exclusive economic zone vest in the Union.",
 w:"Widened by the 40th Amendment in 1976 to cover the exclusive economic zone, which is why offshore oil and gas are Union property regardless of which coast they lie off."},

"298": {p:"The executive power of the Union and of each State extends to carrying on trade or business and to acquiring, holding and disposing of property and making contracts."},

"299": {p:"Every contract made in the exercise of the executive power must be expressed to be made by the President or the Governor, and executed by a person authorised by them — and neither the President nor the Governor is personally liable on it.",
 w:"The requirements are mandatory, not directory: a government contract that does not comply is unenforceable, even if both sides have acted on it. That has caught out a great many contractors."},

"300": {p:"The Union may sue and be sued as the Union of India, and a State in its own name.",
 w:"The article carries forward the old distinction between the sovereign and non-sovereign functions of the State for the purpose of tort liability — a doctrine the Court has steadily narrowed, so that the government today is liable for most of the wrongs its servants commit."},

"300A": {p:"No person shall be deprived of his property save by authority of law.",
 w:"What is left of the right to property after the 44th Amendment moved it out of Part III in 1978. It is a constitutional right but not a fundamental one, so it cannot be enforced under article 32 — though it can under article 226. The Court has given it real content: K. T. Plantation (2011) held the law must be just, fair and reasonable, and Vidya Devi (2020) held that a State cannot take land without acquisition or compensation and then plead limitation."},

/* ------- PART XIII — TRADE, COMMERCE AND INTERCOURSE WITHIN INDIA ------- */

"301": {p:"Trade, commerce and intercourse throughout the territory of India shall be free.",
 w:"India as a single economic space. “Free” does not mean unregulated — the following articles set out who may restrict it and how — but it does mean a State cannot wall itself off. In Atiabari and Automobile Transport the Court settled that only restrictions that directly and immediately impede movement are caught; compensatory taxes for the use of facilities are not."},

"302": {p:"Parliament may impose restrictions on trade between or within States in the public interest."},

"303": {p:"Neither Parliament nor a State legislature may give a preference to one State over another, or discriminate between States, by virtue of an entry on trade and commerce — except that Parliament may do so to deal with a scarcity of goods."},

"304": {p:"A State may tax goods imported from other States as it taxes its own — it may not discriminate — and may impose reasonable restrictions on trade in the public interest, but only with the President's prior sanction.",
 w:"Clause (a) is the non-discrimination rule that keeps entry taxes honest. Clause (b) requires the President's sanction, which is what stops States from raising trade barriers on their own initiative."},

"305": {p:"Existing laws and laws providing for State monopolies are saved from articles 301 and 303.",
 w:"This is why State monopolies in liquor, transport and lotteries survive: the Constitution expressly permits them."},

"306": {p:"Repealed by the 7th Amendment in 1956. It let the Part B States impose trade restrictions under pre-existing agreements."},

"307": {p:"Parliament may by law appoint an authority to carry out the purposes of articles 301 to 304, and confer powers and duties on it.",
 w:"Never used in seventy-five years. The Court in Jindal Stainless (2016) noted the absence of any such authority as one reason this Part has been so heavily litigated."},

/* ------- PART XIV — SERVICES UNDER THE UNION AND THE STATES ------- */

"308": {p:"“State” in this Part does not include Jammu and Kashmir."},

"309": {p:"Parliament or a State legislature may regulate the recruitment and conditions of service of persons in public service — and until they do, the President or Governor may make rules.",
 w:"Almost the whole of Indian service law rests on the proviso rather than the main clause: most conditions of service are governed by executive rules, not by statute, because legislatures have rarely legislated."},

"310": {p:"Every civil servant of the Union holds office during the pleasure of the President, and of a State during the pleasure of the Governor — except as the Constitution expressly provides.",
 w:"The doctrine of pleasure, inherited from the Crown. Article 311 is the exception that makes it bearable."},

"311": {p:"No civil servant may be dismissed or removed by an authority subordinate to the one that appointed them, and none may be dismissed, removed or reduced in rank without an inquiry in which they have been informed of the charges and given a reasonable opportunity to answer. There are three exceptions: conviction on a criminal charge; where an inquiry is not reasonably practicable, for reasons recorded; and where the President or Governor is satisfied that an inquiry would prejudice the security of the State.",
 w:"The security of tenure of the Indian civil service, and the reason a permanent bureaucracy can give unwelcome advice. The 42nd Amendment removed the second opportunity to be heard, on the proposed penalty; Tulsiram Patel (1985) then set out how strictly the three exceptions must be read and confirmed that the reasons are reviewable."},

"312": {p:"If the Rajya Sabha resolves by a two-thirds majority that it is necessary in the national interest, Parliament may create an all-India service common to the Union and the States — and the Indian Administrative Service and the Indian Police Service are deemed to have been created under this article. An all-India judicial service may also be created.",
 w:"The all-India services are the most distinctive feature of Indian administration: officers recruited by the Union but serving in the States, answerable in different ways to both. Note again that it is the Rajya Sabha that must consent. The all-India judicial service in clause (3) was added by the 42nd Amendment and has never been created."},

"312A": {p:"Parliament may vary or revoke the conditions of service of officers appointed by the Secretary of State before independence, and no court may question such a law. Added by the 28th Amendment in 1972, which ended the special protection of the old Indian Civil Service."},

"313": {p:"Until other provision is made, the laws in force before the Constitution about services and posts continue to apply."},

"314": {p:"Repealed. It protected the conditions of service of officers appointed by the Secretary of State before independence. The 28th Amendment removed it in 1972 and put article 312A in its place."},

"315": {p:"There shall be a Public Service Commission for the Union and one for each State — and two or more States may agree to have a Joint Public Service Commission, created by Parliament on their request."},

"316": {p:"Members of a Public Service Commission are appointed by the President or the Governor, and at least half must be people who have held office under the government for at least ten years. They serve six years or until sixty-five at the Union and sixty-two in a State, whichever is earlier."},

"317": {p:"A member of a Public Service Commission can be removed only by the President, and only after the Supreme Court, on a reference, reports that he ought to be removed for misbehaviour. The President may suspend him meanwhile.",
 w:"Removal is by the President even for a State Commission, and only on the Supreme Court's finding — the same architecture of protection used for judges and the CAG."},

"318": {p:"The President or Governor may make regulations on the number of members of a Commission, its staff and their conditions of service."},

"319": {p:"What members may do afterwards: the Chairman of the Union Commission is ineligible for any further government employment; the Chairman of a State Commission may become Chairman or a member of the Union Commission or Chairman of another State Commission, but nothing else.",
 w:"The bar on post-retirement employment is what keeps a recruitment body from being auditioned for its next job by the government it recruits for."},

"320": {p:"Public Service Commissions conduct examinations for appointments, and must be consulted on recruitment methods, promotions, transfers, disciplinary matters and claims for costs incurred defending legal proceedings.",
 w:"Consultation is mandatory but the advice is not binding, and the Court has held that failure to consult does not by itself invalidate an appointment — which considerably weakens the article in practice."},

"321": {p:"Parliament or a State legislature may by law give a Public Service Commission additional functions, including for local authorities and public institutions."},

"322": {p:"The expenses of a Public Service Commission, including salaries and pensions, are charged on the Consolidated Fund of the Union or the State.",
 w:"Charged expenditure again — the budget cannot be voted down."},

"323": {p:"A Public Service Commission must present an annual report to the President or Governor, who must lay it before the legislature together with a memorandum explaining any case where its advice was not accepted.",
 w:"The government may reject the Commission's advice, but must say so in public and explain why."},

/* -------------------------- PART XIVA — TRIBUNALS -------------------------- */

"323A": {p:"Parliament may by law create administrative tribunals for disputes about recruitment and conditions of service of public servants, and may exclude the jurisdiction of all courts except the Supreme Court under article 136.",
 w:"Added by the 42nd Amendment in 1976 and used to create the Central Administrative Tribunal. The exclusion of the High Courts did not survive: L. Chandra Kumar (1997) held that judicial review under articles 226 and 227 is part of the basic structure, so a tribunal's decision must remain open to a High Court bench."},

"323B": {p:"The appropriate legislature may create tribunals for taxation, foreign exchange, industrial and labour disputes, land reform, urban ceiling, elections, foodstuffs and rent — and exclude the jurisdiction of courts.",
 w:"The wider tribunal power, and it is subject to the same limit from L. Chandra Kumar."},

/* -------------------------- PART XV — ELECTIONS -------------------------- */

"324": {p:"The superintendence, direction and control of the preparation of electoral rolls and the conduct of all elections to Parliament, the State legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice-President is vested in an Election Commission. The Chief Election Commissioner may be removed only in the same manner and on the same grounds as a Supreme Court judge, and other Commissioners only on his recommendation.",
 w:"A reservoir of power wide enough to fill gaps in the law — Mohinder Singh Gill (1978) — which is where the model code of conduct and the Commission's power to countermand a poll come from. The protection is uneven: the Chief Election Commissioner has judge-grade security, the other two Commissioners do not. In Anoop Baranwal (2023) the Court ordered appointments by a committee including the Chief Justice; Parliament legislated the following year to replace the Chief Justice with a Union minister."},

"325": {p:"There shall be one general electoral roll for every constituency, and no person may be excluded from it, or claim inclusion in a special roll, on grounds of religion, race, caste or sex.",
 w:"The rejection of separate electorates — the communal representation the colonial government had built and which the Constituent Assembly deliberately abandoned. Reservation of seats under Part XVI is a different thing: reserved constituencies still have a common roll in which everyone votes."},

"326": {p:"Elections to the Lok Sabha and to every State Legislative Assembly are on the basis of adult suffrage: every citizen of eighteen or over who is not disqualified for non-residence, unsoundness of mind, crime or corrupt practice is entitled to be registered.",
 w:"Universal adult suffrage from the first general election, at a moment when most of the world still restricted the vote — and in a country with very low literacy. The age was lowered from twenty-one by the 61st Amendment in 1988."},

"327": {p:"Parliament may make provision by law for all matters relating to elections, including electoral rolls, delimitation and everything necessary to secure the due constitution of the Houses."},

"328": {p:"A State legislature may make provision for elections to its own Houses on matters Parliament has not already provided for."},

"329": {p:"The validity of a delimitation law cannot be questioned in any court, and an election to Parliament or a State legislature may be challenged only by an election petition presented as the law provides.",
 w:"Elections may not be stopped once the process has begun; a grievance is heard afterwards, by the High Court, on an election petition. It is the reason a court will not halt polling."},

"329A": {p:"Repealed. Inserted by the 39th Amendment in 1975 to put the election of the Prime Minister and Speaker beyond the reach of any court, retrospectively, after the Allahabad High Court had set aside Indira Gandhi's election.",
 w:"The Supreme Court struck down its central clause in Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975) — the first time the basic structure doctrine was used to invalidate an amendment. The 44th Amendment then deleted the article entirely in 1978."},

/* ---- PART XVI — SPECIAL PROVISIONS RELATING TO CERTAIN CLASSES ---- */

"330": {p:"Seats are reserved in the Lok Sabha for the Scheduled Castes and the Scheduled Tribes, in proportion to their share of the population of the State or Union territory."},

"330A": {p:"As nearly as may be, one-third of the seats reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in the Lok Sabha, and one-third of the total seats, are reserved for women.",
 w:"Added by the 106th Amendment in 2023 — the women's reservation that had been introduced and failed repeatedly since 1996. It is not yet in force: article 334A makes it wait for the next census and the delimitation that follows."},

"331": {p:"Allowed the President to nominate up to two members of the Anglo-Indian community to the Lok Sabha if he thought them inadequately represented.",
 w:"The nomination lapsed on 25 January 2020, when the 104th Amendment let the special representation of Anglo-Indians expire while extending the SC and ST reservation to 2030."},

"332": {p:"Seats are reserved in every State Legislative Assembly for the Scheduled Castes and the Scheduled Tribes, in proportion to their population."},

"332A": {p:"One-third of the seats in every State Legislative Assembly, including one-third of those reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, are reserved for women. Added by the 106th Amendment in 2023, and subject to the same delay under article 334A."},

"333": {p:"Allowed a Governor to nominate one member of the Anglo-Indian community to a State Legislative Assembly. Lapsed on 25 January 2020 with the 104th Amendment."},

"334": {p:"The reservation of seats for the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes ceases after eighty years from the commencement of the Constitution — that is, in 2030 — and the special representation of Anglo-Indians ceased after seventy years, in 2020.",
 w:"The reservation was originally to last ten years. It has been extended by amendment eight times — the 8th, 23rd, 45th, 62nd, 79th, 95th and 104th — each time for another decade. The 104th extended the SC and ST seats to 2030 but let the Anglo-Indian nominations lapse."},

"334A": {p:"The reservation of seats for women takes effect only after the first census taken after the 106th Amendment came into force, and after the delimitation carried out on the basis of that census — and it lasts fifteen years from then.",
 w:"The article that determines when women's reservation actually starts. Because it depends on a census and then a delimitation, neither of which has a fixed date, the 106th Amendment has no commencement date at all. This site shows none for it, because none has been notified."},

"335": {p:"The claims of the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes must be taken into consideration, consistently with the maintenance of efficiency of administration, in appointments to services — and nothing prevents relaxing qualifying marks or standards of evaluation for reservation in promotion.",
 w:"“Consistently with the maintenance of efficiency of administration” is the phrase that reservation cases turn on. The proviso permitting relaxation of marks was added by the 82nd Amendment in 2000, answering a Supreme Court ruling that had forbidden it."},

"336": {p:"Gave Anglo-Indians a declining share of certain posts in the railways, customs, posts and telegraphs for the first ten years. Long spent."},

"337": {p:"Continued the educational grants that had been made to the Anglo-Indian community, on a declining scale, for the first ten years."},

"338": {p:"There shall be a National Commission for Scheduled Castes — a chairperson, vice-chairperson and three other members — to investigate and monitor safeguards, inquire into complaints, advise on planning, and report annually to the President, who must lay the report before Parliament. It has the powers of a civil court.",
 w:"Upgraded from a Special Officer to a statutory-strength constitutional Commission by the 65th Amendment in 1990, and split from the Scheduled Tribes Commission by the 89th in 2003."},

"338A": {p:"There shall be a National Commission for Scheduled Tribes, with the same composition, powers and duties in relation to the Scheduled Tribes. Added by the 89th Amendment in 2003."},

"338B": {p:"There shall be a National Commission for Backward Classes, with the same structure and powers, and the Union and the States must consult it on all major policy matters affecting socially and educationally backward classes.",
 w:"Added by the 102nd Amendment in 2018. The Commission had existed by statute since 1993, after Indra Sawhney directed that one be set up; this gave it constitutional status."},

"339": {p:"The President may appoint a Commission to report on the administration of the Scheduled Areas and the welfare of the Scheduled Tribes, and the Union may direct a State on schemes for tribal welfare."},

"340": {p:"The President may appoint a Commission to investigate the conditions of socially and educationally backward classes and recommend what should be done for them, and the report must be laid before Parliament with a memorandum of the action taken.",
 w:"The article under which the Kalelkar Commission was appointed in 1953 and the Mandal Commission in 1979. The Mandal report's recommendation of 27 per cent reservation, implemented in 1990, produced Indra Sawhney."},

"341": {p:"The President may, by public notification, specify the castes, races or tribes that shall be deemed Scheduled Castes in relation to a State or Union territory — in consultation with the Governor — and only Parliament may then add to or remove from that list.",
 w:"The list is the President's to make and Parliament's to alter; a State cannot change it. That is why demands for inclusion turn into central legislation."},

"342": {p:"The same mechanism for the Scheduled Tribes: presidential notification, and alteration only by Parliament."},

"342A": {p:"The President specifies the socially and educationally backward classes for the central list, and only Parliament may alter it — but every State may prepare and maintain its own list of such classes for its own purposes.",
 w:"Added by the 102nd Amendment in 2018. In 2021 the Supreme Court read it as taking away the States' power to identify backward classes; Parliament reversed that within months through the 105th Amendment, which added the clause expressly preserving State lists."},

/* --------------------- PART XVII — OFFICIAL LANGUAGE --------------------- */

"343": {p:"The official language of the Union is Hindi in the Devanagari script, with international numerals. English was to continue for fifteen years from 1950 — but Parliament may provide by law for its continued use beyond that.",
 w:"The fifteen-year deadline expired in 1965 and set off violent protests in Tamil Nadu. Parliament had already passed the Official Languages Act, 1963, and amended it in 1967 to guarantee that English would continue for as long as any non-Hindi-speaking State wanted it. Hindi is an official language of the Union; it has never been the national language, a distinction the Constitution does not make anywhere."},

"344": {p:"The President must constitute a Commission on official language after five years and again after ten, and a Committee of thirty members of Parliament to examine its recommendations and report to him."},

"345": {p:"A State legislature may adopt any language in use in the State, or Hindi, as its official language — and until it does, English continues."},

"346": {p:"The language authorised for use in the Union is the language for communication between States and between a State and the Union, though two States may agree to use Hindi between themselves."},

"347": {p:"If a substantial proportion of a State's population wants a language they speak to be recognised, the President may direct that it be officially recognised throughout that State or a part of it."},

"348": {p:"Proceedings in the Supreme Court and the High Courts, and the authoritative texts of all Bills, Acts, ordinances, orders, rules and regulations, are in English — until Parliament provides otherwise. A Governor may, with the President's consent, authorise Hindi or another language in the High Court, but not for judgments unless Parliament so provides.",
 w:"This is why the law of India is authoritative in English. Judgments of a High Court may be delivered in another language only with the President's consent, and several States have sought it; the Supreme Court has never operated in any language but English."},

"349": {p:"For the first fifteen years, a Bill changing the language used in the courts or in legislation could not be introduced without the President's previous sanction, given after considering the Commission's recommendations. Spent."},

"350": {p:"Every person may submit a representation for the redress of a grievance to any officer or authority of the Union or a State in any language used in the Union or the State.",
 w:"A right of every person, not only citizens, and it does not depend on the language being an official one."},

"350A": {p:"Every State and local authority must endeavour to provide adequate facilities for instruction in the mother tongue at the primary stage to children of linguistic minority groups. Added by the 7th Amendment in 1956."},

"350B": {p:"There shall be a Special Officer for linguistic minorities, appointed by the President, to investigate all matters relating to their safeguards and report to him, and the report is laid before Parliament and sent to the States. Added by the 7th Amendment in 1956.",
 w:"A recommendation of the States Reorganisation Commission: once States were drawn on linguistic lines, every State acquired linguistic minorities of its own."},

"351": {p:"It is the duty of the Union to promote the spread of Hindi so that it may serve as a medium of expression for the composite culture of India — drawing on Hindustani and the other languages of the Eighth Schedule, and primarily on Sanskrit for its vocabulary.",
 w:"A directive to the Union, not a power over anyone. Its instruction to enrich Hindi from Sanskrit and from the Eighth Schedule languages is the constitutional source of the official Hindi vocabulary."},

/* --------------------- PART XVIII — EMERGENCY PROVISIONS --------------------- */

"352": {p:"If the President is satisfied that the security of India or any part of it is threatened by war, external aggression or armed rebellion, he may proclaim an emergency — but only on the written advice of the Cabinet. The proclamation must be approved by both Houses within a month, by a majority of the total membership and two-thirds of those present and voting, and then lasts six months at a time. The Lok Sabha can revoke it by a simple majority if a tenth of its members demand a sitting.",
 w:"Almost every safeguard here was written by the 44th Amendment in 1978, in direct response to 1975. The original ground was “internal disturbance”, which is what was invoked in June 1975; it was replaced with the far narrower “armed rebellion”. The requirement of written Cabinet advice, the one-month approval window, the six-month renewal, and the Lok Sabha's power to force a revocation are all post-emergency additions. A national emergency has been proclaimed three times: 1962, 1971 and 1975."},

"353": {p:"While an emergency is in force, the Union's executive power extends to directing a State on how to exercise its own executive power, and Parliament may legislate on State subjects.",
 w:"This is the clause that converts the federation into a unitary state for the duration."},

"354": {p:"The President may modify the constitutional provisions on the distribution of revenues between the Union and the States while an emergency is in force, until the end of the financial year in which it ceases."},

"355": {p:"It is the duty of the Union to protect every State against external aggression and internal disturbance, and to ensure that the government of every State is carried on in accordance with the Constitution.",
 w:"A duty, and it is the constitutional justification for Union action in a State. In S. R. Bommai the Court read it as imposing an obligation that must be discharged by means short of article 356 wherever possible."},

"356": {p:"If the President is satisfied, on a Governor's report or otherwise, that the government of a State cannot be carried on in accordance with the Constitution, he may assume the State's functions to himself, declare that Parliament shall exercise the State legislature's powers, and suspend any constitutional provision relating to any State body — but not the powers of the High Court. The proclamation must be approved by both Houses within two months, lasts six months at a time, and cannot normally run beyond three years.",
 w:"President's rule, and the most abused article in the Constitution: used more than a hundred and thirty times, often to dismiss governments of a different party. S. R. Bommai (1994) changed that. It held the proclamation is subject to judicial review, that the majority of a government must be tested on the floor of the House rather than judged by the Governor, and that an Assembly cannot be dissolved until Parliament has approved — so a dismissal can be undone. Its use has fallen sharply since."},

"357": {p:"When Parliament takes over a State legislature's powers, it may confer them on the President, who may delegate them, and any law so made continues until altered by the State legislature once it is restored."},

"358": {p:"While an emergency declared on the ground of war or external aggression is in force, article 19 is suspended: the State may make any law or take any action that would otherwise offend it.",
 w:"Note the limit written in by the 44th Amendment: this applies only to an emergency proclaimed on the ground of war or external aggression, not armed rebellion, and only to laws that expressly recite their connection to the emergency."},

"359": {p:"During an emergency the President may by order suspend the right to move any court for the enforcement of the rights in Part III — except articles 20 and 21 — for the period stated in the order.",
 w:"The exception for articles 20 and 21 is the direct answer to ADM Jabalpur (1976), where the Court held that during an emergency no one could approach a court even against an unlawful detention. The 44th Amendment made those two rights unsuspendable, so that judgment could never be repeated. The Court itself formally overruled it in Puttaswamy (2017)."},

"359A": {p:"Repealed. Inserted by the 59th Amendment in 1988 to allow an emergency in Punjab on the ground of internal disturbance, and President's rule there for up to three years. The 63rd Amendment removed it in 1990."},

"360": {p:"If the President is satisfied that the financial stability or credit of India is threatened, he may proclaim a financial emergency. The Union may then direct States on financial propriety, reduce the salaries of all public servants including judges of the Supreme Court and the High Courts, and require all State money Bills to be reserved for the President.",
 w:"Never used. It is the only provision in the Constitution that permits judges' salaries to be cut."},

/* ----------------------- PART XIX — MISCELLANEOUS ----------------------- */

"361": {p:"The President and the Governors are not answerable to any court for the exercise of their powers. No criminal proceedings may be started or continued against them while in office, no process for their arrest may issue, and civil proceedings require two months' notice.",
 w:"Personal immunity for the duration of office, not a permanent one — and it does not bar impeachment, nor a court from reviewing the acts done, as distinct from the officeholder."},

"361A": {p:"No one may be sued for publishing a substantially true report of the proceedings of Parliament or a State legislature, unless the publication is proved to have been made with malice — but this does not protect the publication of a secret sitting. Added by the 44th Amendment in 1978.",
 w:"Passed after the emergency, when press coverage of Parliament had been curtailed. It gives reporting of the legislature a statutory-strength privilege."},

"361B": {p:"A member disqualified for defection under the Tenth Schedule cannot hold any remunerative political post until his term expires or he is re-elected. Added by the 91st Amendment in 2003.",
 w:"Closing the loophole that let a defector who lost his seat be rewarded with a chairmanship of a public corporation."},

"362": {p:"Repealed. It required the rights and privileges of the former rulers to be respected in making laws. The 26th Amendment abolished it in 1971 along with the privy purses."},

"363": {p:"Courts cannot adjudicate disputes arising out of treaties, agreements, covenants and similar instruments entered into before the Constitution by the rulers of Indian States.",
 w:"It closes the courts to claims founded on the instruments of accession themselves."},

"363A": {p:"The recognition of the rulers of the former princely states is abolished, and the privy purses are extinguished. Added by the 26th Amendment in 1971.",
 w:"The operative article of the privy purse abolition — article 291 and article 362 were repealed, and this put the abolition positively."},

"364": {p:"The President may, by notification, direct that an Act of Parliament or of a State legislature does not apply, or applies with modifications, to a major port or aerodrome."},

"365": {p:"If a State fails to comply with a direction given by the Union, the President may hold that a situation has arisen in which the government of the State cannot be carried on in accordance with the Constitution.",
 w:"The bridge from articles 256 and 257 to article 356. It converts a failure to obey a Union direction into a ground for President's rule — which is why the direction power is not a small thing."},

"366": {p:"The definitions article: thirty definitions used throughout the Constitution, from “agricultural income” and “Anglo-Indian” to “goods and services tax”, “Scheduled Castes”, “taxation” and “Union territory”.",
 w:"Worth reading once. The definition of “goods and services tax” was added by the 101st Amendment, and “Scheduled Castes” and “Scheduled Tribes” point back to articles 341 and 342."},

"367": {p:"Unless the context otherwise requires, the General Clauses Act, 1897 applies to the interpretation of the Constitution as it does to an Act of Parliament.",
 w:"A quiet article that became decisive in 2019: the presidential order on Jammu and Kashmir used article 367 to redefine what “Constituent Assembly of the State” meant in article 370, and the Supreme Court in 2023 held that route impermissible while upholding the outcome on other grounds."},

/* --------------- PART XX — AMENDMENT OF THE CONSTITUTION --------------- */

"368": {p:"Parliament may amend the Constitution by adding, changing or repealing any provision. A Bill needs a majority of the total membership of each House and two-thirds of those present and voting. Changes touching the federal structure — the election of the President, the distribution of legislative powers, the Seventh Schedule, the representation of States in Parliament, or this article itself — also need ratification by the legislatures of half the States. The President must then assent.",
 w:"The whole constitutional argument of the twentieth century lives in this article. Shankari Prasad (1951) and Sajjan Singh (1965) said the power was unlimited; Golak Nath (1967) said fundamental rights were beyond it; the 24th Amendment overrode Golak Nath; and Kesavananda Bharati (1973) settled it — Parliament may amend anything, but may not damage the basic structure. The 42nd Amendment then inserted clauses (4) and (5) declaring the power unlimited and beyond review, and Minerva Mills (1980) struck both down. The official text still prints them, with a footnote recording that they are invalid. Note what is not required: no referendum, and no ratification by more than half the States."},

/* ------- PART XXI — TEMPORARY, TRANSITIONAL AND SPECIAL PROVISIONS ------- */

"369": {p:"Gave Parliament temporary power, for five years from 1950, to legislate on certain State List subjects — trade in essential commodities, and some offences and procedure — as if they were in the Concurrent List. Spent."},

"370": {p:"The temporary provision that gave Jammu and Kashmir a special constitutional position: Parliament's power to legislate for it was limited to defence, external affairs and communications, and other provisions of the Constitution applied only with the concurrence of the State government.",
 w:"On 5 August 2019 a presidential order under this article applied the whole Constitution to Jammu and Kashmir, and a second declared that article 370 had ceased to operate; the State was then reorganised into two Union territories. In In re Article 370 (2023) a constitution bench upheld both, holding that the article was always temporary and that the State retained no internal sovereignty — while directing that statehood be restored and elections held. It is the only article whose marginal heading still calls it temporary and which remains, formally, in the text."},

"371": {p:"Lets the President give the Governors of Maharashtra and Gujarat special responsibility for setting up development boards for Vidarbha, Marathwada, the rest of Maharashtra, and Saurashtra, Kutch and the rest of Gujarat — with equitable allocation of funds and of opportunities in technical education, training and employment.",
 w:"The first of the eleven special-provision articles for particular States. Together, articles 371 to 371J are the Constitution's admission that a uniform design does not fit every region."},

"371A": {p:"No Act of Parliament on Naga religious or social practices, Naga customary law and procedure, the administration of civil and criminal justice according to Naga customary law, or the ownership and transfer of land and its resources applies to Nagaland unless its Legislative Assembly so decides. The Governor has special responsibility for law and order while internal disturbances continue.",
 w:"The strongest protection any State has. It gives the Nagaland Assembly a veto over Parliament in the listed subjects — a genuine carve-out from article 246. Added by the 13th Amendment in 1962 as part of the settlement that created the State."},

"371B": {p:"The President may provide for a committee of the Assam Legislative Assembly consisting of members elected from the tribal areas. Added by the 22nd Amendment in 1969."},

"371C": {p:"The President may provide for a committee of the Manipur Legislative Assembly consisting of members elected from the hill areas, and give the Governor special responsibility for their administration. Added by the 27th Amendment in 1971."},

"371D": {p:"Requires equitable opportunities in public employment and education for people from different parts of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, with local cadres, an Administrative Tribunal, and the President's power to make orders that override anything else.",
 w:"Added by the 32nd Amendment in 1973 after the agitations over the Telangana region, and extended to both States after the 2014 bifurcation."},

"371E": {p:"Parliament may by law provide for the establishment of a university in Andhra Pradesh.",
 w:"One sentence, and the shortest of the special provisions. The University of Hyderabad was established under it."},

"371F": {p:"The special terms on which Sikkim joined India in 1975: the size and duration of its first Assembly, seats reserved for sections of its population, the Governor's special responsibility for peace and equitable arrangements, and the continuance of its pre-merger laws.",
 w:"Added by the 36th Amendment. Clause (k) continuing the old laws is why Sikkim retains its own income-tax arrangements."},

"371G": {p:"No Act of Parliament on Mizo religious or social practices, Mizo customary law and procedure, the administration of justice according to it, or the ownership and transfer of land applies to Mizoram unless its Assembly so decides. Added by the 53rd Amendment in 1986.",
 w:"Modelled directly on article 371A, as part of the Mizo Accord."},

"371H": {p:"Gives the Governor of Arunachal Pradesh special responsibility for law and order, exercised in his individual judgment after consulting the council of ministers, until the President directs otherwise. Added by the 55th Amendment in 1986."},

"371-I": {p:"The Legislative Assembly of Goa shall have not less than thirty members. Added by the 56th Amendment in 1987.",
 w:"The shortest special provision in the Constitution: a single guarantee of a minimum Assembly size for a very small State."},

"371J": {p:"Gives the Governor of Karnataka special responsibility for the development of the Hyderabad-Karnataka region — a development board, equitable allocation of funds, and reservation in education and State employment for people of the region. Added by the 98th Amendment in 2012.",
 w:"The most recent of the special provisions, and the only one that provides reservation on the basis of region rather than caste or tribe."},

"372": {p:"All laws in force before the Constitution continue until altered, repealed or amended, and the President could adapt them to bring them into conformity with the Constitution.",
 w:"The article that stopped the legal system from vanishing on 26 January 1950. Everything from the Indian Penal Code to the Transfer of Property Act survived through this one provision."},

"372A": {p:"Gave the President power to adapt laws to bring them into conformity with the Constitution as amended by the 7th Amendment, up to 1 November 1957. Added by the 7th Amendment in 1956 to cope with the reorganisation of the States."},

"373": {p:"Let the President make orders about preventive detention until Parliament legislated. Spent."},

"374": {p:"Made the judges of the old Federal Court judges of the Supreme Court, and transferred its pending proceedings and the appeals then before the Privy Council.",
 w:"The article by which the Supreme Court inherited its predecessor — and by which appeals to the Privy Council in London finally ended."},

"375": {p:"All courts, authorities and officers continued to function, subject to the Constitution."},

"376": {p:"The judges of the old High Courts became judges of the High Courts under the Constitution."},

"377": {p:"The Auditor-General of India became the first Comptroller and Auditor-General under the Constitution."},

"378": {p:"The members of the old Public Service Commissions continued as members of the new ones."},

"378A": {p:"Fixed the duration of the first Andhra Pradesh Legislative Assembly, constituted in 1953. Spent."},

"392": {p:"Gave the President power, until the first Parliament met, to remove difficulties in the transition by order — including by adapting the Constitution itself.",
 w:"A remarkable power: for a short window, the President could modify the Constitution by order to make the transition work. It expired with the first sitting of Parliament."},

/* ---- PART XXII — SHORT TITLE, COMMENCEMENT, HINDI TEXT AND REPEALS ---- */

"393": {p:"This Constitution may be called the Constitution of India."},

"394": {p:"Articles 5 to 9, 60, 324, 366, 367, 379 to 391, 392 and this article came into force on 26 November 1949; the rest on 26 January 1950.",
 w:"Two dates, and both matter. The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 — Constitution Day — and the machinery needed to bring a country into being started at once. The rest waited until 26 January 1950, chosen because the Congress had declared purna swaraj on that day twenty years earlier."},

"394A": {p:"The President shall publish an authoritative translation of the Constitution in Hindi, and further translations of every amendment, and such a text shall have the same meaning as the original. Added by the 58th Amendment in 1987.",
 w:"Until 1987 there was no Hindi text with legal force. Note the careful wording: if the Hindi text presents a difficulty, the President must issue a revised translation — the English text remains the reference."},

"395": {p:"Repeals the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935, with everything amending them.",
 w:"The last article, and the one that cuts the cord. With it, the legal authority of the Indian state stops deriving from an Act of the British Parliament and starts deriving from the Constitution itself — from the people of India, as the Preamble says."}

});
